      <h1>What is UK Stamp Duty Land Tax (SDLT)?</h1>
      <p class="meta">Written by the Engineering Team at RevoCalc | Updated for 2026</p>

      <p>If you are buying a property in England or Northern Ireland, you will almost certainly encounter Stamp Duty Land Tax (SDLT). It is one of the largest upfront costs of buying a home, yet many buyers don't understand how it is calculated until they receive a bill from their solicitor.</p>

      <p>In this comprehensive 2026 guide, we will break down exactly what SDLT is, how the tiered tax bands work, and how you can calculate your exact bill before you put in an offer.</p>

      <h2>What is Stamp Duty?</h2>
      <p>Stamp Duty is a tax paid to the UK government when you purchase a property or land over a certain value. It was historically a tax on physical documents (hence the "stamp"), but today it is a digital transaction tax handled by your solicitor or conveyancer.</p>
      
      <p>To calculate your exact bill instantly without doing the manual math, use our free <a href="/tools/uk-stamp-duty-calculator">UK Stamp Duty Calculator</a>.</p>

      <h2>What Price Does Stamp Duty Start?</h2>
      <p>For standard buyers (home movers), Stamp Duty starts at properties priced over <strong>£250,000</strong>. If the property is £250,000 or less, you pay zero Stamp Duty.</p>
      <p>However, if you are a first-time buyer, the starting threshold is much higher: you pay zero Stamp Duty until the property price exceeds <strong>£425,000</strong>.</p>

      <h2>How Does Stamp Duty Work? (The Math)</h2>
      <p>SDLT is a <strong>marginal tax</strong>. This is a crucial concept. You do not pay a flat rate on the entire property price. Instead, the price is broken into bands, and you pay a percentage on the portion of the price that falls within each band.</p>
      
      <p>Here are the standard 2026 rates for a single property (England & Northern Ireland):</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>£0 to £250,000:</strong> 0%</li>
        <li><strong>£250,001 to £925,000:</strong> 5%</li>
        <li><strong>£925,001 to £1.5 million:</strong> 10%</li>
        <li><strong>Over £1.5 million:</strong> 12%</li>
      </ul>

      <p><strong>The Math Example:</strong> You buy a house for £300,000. You pay 0% on the first £250,000, and 5% on the remaining £50,000. Total SDLT = £2,500. (Not 5% of £300,000, which would be £15,000).</p>

      <h2>First-Time Buyer Relief</h2>
      <p>The government offers massive relief to first-time buyers to help them get on the property ladder. If you are a first-time buyer, the bands change:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>£0 to £425,000:</strong> 0%</li>
        <li><strong>£425,001 to £625,000:</strong> 5%</li>
      </ul>
      <p>If the property costs more than £625,000, you lose the first-time buyer relief entirely and must pay standard rates. This is often called the "cliff edge" – buying a house for £626,000 can actually result in a much higher tax bill than buying one for £624,000.</p>

      <h2>When is Stamp Duty Land Tax Payable?</h2>
      <p>You must pay your SDLT bill within <strong>14 days</strong> of completing your property purchase. If you fail to pay on time, HMRC can charge you penalties and interest. Because of this tight deadline, it is always handled on completion day.</p>

      <h2>How is Stamp Duty Paid?</h2>
      <p>You do not pay HMRC directly. Your solicitor or conveyancer will file the SDLT return to HMRC and pay the tax on your behalf. The funds for the tax are usually taken from the total money you transfer to your solicitor for completion. You will see the Stamp Duty amount listed clearly on your final completion statement.</p>

      <h2>What About Buy-to-Let or Second Homes?</h2>
      <p>If you are buying an additional property (like a buy-to-let or a holiday home), you must pay a 3% surcharge on top of the standard rates. This was introduced to cool down the property market and help first-time buyers. You can use our calculator to factor in this surcharge.</p>

      <h2>Frequently Asked Questions (FAQ)</h2>
      
      <h3>Do I pay Stamp Duty if I am buying in Scotland or Wales?</h3>
      <p>No. Scotland has the Land and Buildings Transaction Tax (LBTT), and Wales has the Land Transaction Tax (LTT). They have different bands and rules. Our calculator currently covers England and Northern Ireland.</p>
      
      <h3>Can I add Stamp Duty to my mortgage?</h3>
      <p>No, you cannot borrow money from your mortgage lender to pay Stamp Duty. It must be paid in cash from your own savings.</p>

      <h3>What happens if I overpay my Stamp Duty?</h3>
      <p>If you realize you have overpaid SDLT (for example, if you were entitled to relief but didn't claim it), you can apply to HMRC for a refund within 12 months of the filing date.</p>
